import{_ as s,o as a,c as r,ao as t}from"./chunks/framework.CJX_rJCp.js";const o="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l1._rfPDj0-.svg",l="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l2.C7MbdXIa.svg",p="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l3.D9A1Tr7A.svg",n="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_lapack.DCc5cB9A.svg",c="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl1.BBYRLHaN.svg",i="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl2.DVaXdqjq.svg",_="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl3.DKJN8La-.svg",d="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_clapack.BDp6JTXR.svg",m="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l1_aocl.DE37xRaH.svg",h="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l2_aocl.C9Kt0xdM.svg",v="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_l3_aocl.BccHEE95.svg",f="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_lapack_aocl.Bd7CUOnV.svg",g="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl1_aocl.Cn-Fe-8u.svg",u="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl2_aocl.D-oGN7vQ.svg",A="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_cl3_aocl.DWpNwfhA.svg",L="/PureBLAS.jl/dev/assets/perf_clapack_aocl.DK5bGqNb.svg",D=JSON.parse('{"title":"Performance","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"performance.md","filePath":"performance.md","lastUpdated":null}'),b={name:"performance.md"};function B(P,e,S,j,C,O){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<h1 id="Performance" tabindex="-1">Performance <a class="header-anchor" href="#Performance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performance {#Performance}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Each plot shows how fast PureBLAS is relative to a reference, against problem size. Higher is better and the dashed line is parity, so anything above it is a win. There is one curve per microarchitecture (Zen3 · AVX2, Zen4 · AVX-512, Zen5 · AVX-512) and one panel per operation, and the band around a curve is the q10–q90 spread of the pooled per-round ratios. Everything is single-threaded, Float64 and ComplexF64.</p><p>Roughly 87% of the measured cells are at or above <code>max(OpenBLAS, AOCL)</code>. The ones that are not are listed in <a href="./notes">Notes</a>, and most of them sit within a few percent.</p><p>For the numbers behind the plots, see <a href="https://github.com/el-oso/PureBLAS.jl/blob/master/bench/gen_table.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>bench/gen_table.md</code></a> against OpenBLAS and <a href="https://github.com/el-oso/PureBLAS.jl/blob/master/bench/gen_table_aocl.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>bench/gen_table_aocl.md</code></a> against AOCL. <a href="./methodology">Methodology</a> covers how the measurements are taken and how to read a cell; <a href="./notes">Notes</a> has the per-routine analysis and history.</p><h2 id="vs-OpenBLAS" tabindex="-1">vs OpenBLAS <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-OpenBLAS" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs OpenBLAS {#vs-OpenBLAS}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+o+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+l+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+p+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+n+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+c+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+i+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+_+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+d+'" alt=""></p><h2 id="vs-AOCL" tabindex="-1">vs AOCL <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-AOCL" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs AOCL {#vs-AOCL}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+m+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+h+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+v+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+f+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+g+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+u+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+A+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+L+'" alt=""></p><p>These numbers apply however you call PureBLAS. The native API (<code>PureBLAS.gemm!(…)</code>), the in-process reroute through <code>activate()</code>, and the <code>libpureblas.so</code> built for C hosts all run the same kernels.</p>',23)])])}const T=s(b,[["render",B]]);export{D as __pageData,T as default};
